sdspisim: size the card from the image it was given

Two things stop SDSPISIM reporting the capacity of the file behind it,
and both are visible the moment a host reads the CSD and believes it.

CSD() is called from the constructor, before load() has opened the image
and worked out how many sectors it holds, and it is never called again.
So m_devblocks is zero when the CSD is built and every card reports what
a block count of zero encodes, whatever image is loaded afterwards.

And C_SIZE in a version 2 CSD is not a sector count.  The capacity is
(C_SIZE + 1) * 512 KB - (C_SIZE + 1) * 1024 sectors - so writing the
sector count into the field overstates the card by a factor of 1024.
A host that reads it the way the specification defines it gets a disk
1024 times too big, which is the kind of wrong that only shows up when a
filesystem runs off the end.

Found by driving this model and an independent SystemVerilog one through
the same initialisation and comparing what each said its capacity was:
the Verilog host computed 1024 sectors from this model's CSD and 2048
from the other's, against a 2048-sector image.

Not sent upstream: it is a fix to a simulation model this project only
borrows, and the maintainer may have reasons for the shape it has.

---
diff --git a/bench/cpp/sdspisim.cpp b/bench/cpp/sdspisim.cpp
index 8f2d492..6099030 100644
--- a/bench/cpp/sdspisim.cpp
+++ b/bench/cpp/sdspisim.cpp
@@ -94,6 +94,11 @@ void	SDSPISIM::load(const char *fname) {
 
 		m_devblocks = devln>>LGSECTOR_SIZE;
 
+		// The CSD is built in the constructor, before this is known, so
+		// without this every card reports whatever a block count of zero
+		// encodes - 512 KB - however big the image is.
+		CSD();
+
 		if (m_debug) printf("SDCARD: NBLOCKS = %ld\n", m_devblocks);
 	}
 }
@@ -169,9 +174,16 @@ void	SDSPISIM::CSD(void) {
 	// The following code works on high-capacity cards only
 	assert((DEFAULT_CSD[0] & 0x0c0)== 0x40);
 
-	m_csd[7] = (m_devblocks >> 16) & 0x03f;
-	m_csd[8] = (m_devblocks >>  8) & 0x0ff;
-	m_csd[9] = (m_devblocks      ) & 0x0ff;
+	// C_SIZE in a version 2 CSD is a count of 512 KB units, not of
+	// sectors: the capacity is (C_SIZE + 1) * 512 KB, which is
+	// (C_SIZE + 1) * 1024 sectors of 512 bytes.  Writing the sector
+	// count here directly overstates the card by a factor of 1024.
+	unsigned long	c_size;
+
+	c_size = (m_devblocks >= 1024) ? (m_devblocks / 1024) - 1 : 0;
+	m_csd[7] = (c_size >> 16) & 0x03f;
+	m_csd[8] = (c_size >>  8) & 0x0ff;
+	m_csd[9] = (c_size      ) & 0x0ff;
 
 	//
 	// Now make the CRC match
